1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
probably a lot of the story is something like that.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:05,680
And also notice that the border doesn't move even a foot a year.

3
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:07,200
The border just stays exactly there.

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:11,600
There's something very sacrosanct about that little line

5
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,680
so that if one country were to say, look,

6
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:16,560
we just want to move it one foot north, that's all we're asking.

7
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:19,280
That by itself could be something that broke a war.

8
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:21,160
It seems, in fact, pretty plausible.

9
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:24,240
If you wanted to go and get a war with Canada,

10
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:26,080
for the smallest possible cause, just saying

11
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:28,640
we want to move the border one foot north

12
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:31,160
seems like a pretty good way to do it.

13
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:33,120
That's what's going on in Kashmir.

14
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:36,720
They're debating about what is the sovereign territory.

15
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:39,800
So if one person is crossing that sovereign territory,

16
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:41,160
they're fighting over the whole.

17
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:42,080
Right, right.

18
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:42,640
Yes.

19
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:44,280
I mean, the US-Canadian case is more interesting

20
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:45,760
just because it's had, there's been

21
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:47,040
peace for over 100 years.

22
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:49,640
There's agreement on what the border.

23
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:51,440
Right, right.

24
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:53,280
All right.

25
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:55,280
So anyway, so people are more likely to fight

26
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:58,000
and attack their own territory than the expectation.

27
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,480
Makes the threat to fight or attack

28
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:01,760
more credible than the threat to fight if resisted.

29
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,200
Yeah, and actually, the main point

30
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:05,480
of the peace with their camera is on the black market.

31
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:07,560
So black markets are interesting because not only

32
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:09,720
is this property the government doesn't respect,

33
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:14,080
it's generally property the government says is illegal.

34
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:16,640
And yet, how many movies about drugs

35
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:18,400
have been based on the premise that someone

36
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:20,400
stole somebody's drugs?

37
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:21,800
Right, you can't go to the police

38
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:24,840
and say they stole my drugs, or they'll arrest you.

39
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:27,120
Right, and yet, there is a sense that if you

40
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:28,680
want to get in trouble with the mafia,

41
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:31,400
one good bet is to go and steal some of their drugs.

42
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:33,200
You say, well, if it's not recognized,

43
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:35,200
it's recognized by us.

44
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:39,000
So you better watch it.

45
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,600
All right, questions, questions?

46
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:43,240
All right.

47
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:45,240
So and then the last topic.

48
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:47,720
So rent-seeking and lobbying inefficiency.

49
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:50,320
Remember, we already talked about two kinds

50
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:51,120
of inefficiencies.

51
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:53,000
We talked about allocative inefficiency

52
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,440
and productive inefficiency.

53
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:57,440
All right.

54
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:03,640
So the allocative inefficiency is

55
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:06,280
the kind that gets almost all attention in textbooks,

56
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:08,120
especially I.O. textbooks.

57
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:11,560
So where you start off with this is your marital cost,

58
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:14,640
this is demand, and then we've got a monopoly here.

59
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:17,040
So we are pricing up here.

60
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:20,960
And then we have this triangle of deadweight loss,

61
00:02:20,960 --> 00:02:24,640
which is the allocative inefficiency.

62
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:29,680
And then we talked about the further inefficiency caused

63
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:34,960
by having costs higher than they socially need to be.

64
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:38,160
So for example, this could be the marginal cost

65
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:40,920
of the brother-in-law of the dictator.

66
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:44,640
But here is the marginal cost of the most efficient firm.

67
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:46,160
And then as we saw, there's going

68
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:48,800
to be another set of inefficiency

69
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:51,760
here in the shape of a trapezoid.

70
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:55,240
And this is the productive inefficiency.

71
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:57,600
So this is all the inefficiency associated

72
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:01,400
with the fact that costs are higher than they socially

73
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:01,840
need to be.

74
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:07,320
So we've got a trapezoid and we've

75
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:09,600
got a triangle inefficiency.

76
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:12,600
Now, there is another kind of inefficiency

77
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:15,000
that Gordon Tullock is famously associated with.

78
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,480
Although there's a bunch of other people that followed up

79
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:20,040
on him as to whether or not they were actually

80
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:22,800
copying him and republishing or whether they're

81
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:23,760
independent discoverers.

82
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:25,200
We may never know.

83
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:27,640
But in any case, final form of inefficiency

84
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:31,360
usually known as lobbying or rent-seeking inefficiency.

85
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:34,880
So it arises when people use resources to cause

86
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:37,320
the transfer of resources.

87
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:40,680
So when people actually spend something

88
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:45,920
in order to gain control of a fixed pie of stuff.

89
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:48,760
And the simplest example would be something like this.

90
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:52,200
So suppose that rather than the brother-in-law just

91
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,560
getting the monopoly for free, instead you

92
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:58,560
have to go and fight in the legislature.

93
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:01,880
So you have to go and hire a bunch of lawyers

94
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:04,320
and you have to bribe a bunch of congressmen.

95
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:08,400
Or you have to go and hire some researchers to argue

96
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:09,520
on your behalf.

97
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:12,680
Or maybe you need to go and take out a big advertising campaign.

98
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:14,480
So you have to put up resources in order

99
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:17,160
to become the monopolist.

100
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:22,560
Now in the simple model, this rectangle here is what?

101
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:24,200
In the simple, so not with rent-seeking,

102
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:25,680
but the very simple story monopoly,

103
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:27,280
what is that rectangle?

104
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:28,200
Profit.

105
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:28,760
Yeah, profit.

106
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:31,400
That rectangle is normally monopoly profits.

107
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:35,160
But if you get this monopoly profit

108
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:37,080
by becoming a monopolist, there is

109
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:38,800
a reason to spend resources in order

110
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:40,640
to get that monopoly profit.

111
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:44,760
And so this goes from being all monopoly profit to just

112
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:46,720
being the gross monopoly profit.

113
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:49,920
It's a monopoly profit that you get if you win.

114
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:51,480
But in order to win, you might have

115
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:55,240
to spend a lot of resources.

116
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:57,000
And those resources get burned up.

117
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,080
They're not actually going to be enjoyed by anyone in society.

118
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:06,680
So just imagine a world where the more you spend

119
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:09,080
on your lobbying efforts to convince the world

120
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:11,080
that you deserve to be a monopolist,

121
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:14,200
the more you prove your chances.

122
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:17,400
And we can picture this like a tug-of-war contest.

123
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:20,040
So the game tug-of-war where there's a rope,

124
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:22,120
and then there's a thing of mud in the center.

125
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:26,000
And then you have people more pulling the rope.

126
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:31,400
And then imagine that each side can go and hire

127
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:34,240
more assistant rope pullers.

128
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:36,280
And you can end, you know, that's really bad.

129
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:37,280
Like, it's not just mud.

130
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:39,960
Maybe it's like molten lava.

131
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:42,280
All right, so you can keep adding more and more people.

132
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:44,800
Each side will keep adding more and more people.

133
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:52,600
And when in equilibrium does the expected benefits

134
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:55,840
of participating in this process go down to just the normal

135
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:58,160
level?

136
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:00,120
So anyway, Gordon Tollick's deep insight here

137
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:02,280
is we can think about this as just

138
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:04,480
being a competitive industry.

139
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:06,400
In competitive industry, in the long run,

140
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,160
price is equal to what again?

141
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:09,320
Yeah, margin loss.

142
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:13,320
But also, more importantly in this case, average cost.

143
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:15,840
So you keep getting entry in the long run

144
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:19,360
until price is going to be equal to average cost.

145
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:23,600
So what this means is that if initially the rate of return

146
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:26,280
to fighting over those monopoly profits is 10%,

147
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:27,840
this will give you more entry.

148
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:29,880
And then finally, you will get entry

149
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:34,080
until the rate of return in the lobbying industry.

150
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,040
We'll call it industry metaphorically

151
00:06:36,040 --> 00:06:38,480
because it's not very industrious.

152
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:40,480
It doesn't lead to production, but it

153
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:42,760
does lead to redistribution.

154
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:47,760
You get more and more entry until our economic profits are

155
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:48,640
what?

156
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:49,160
Zero.

157
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:52,480
Yes, economic profits are zero.

158
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:53,320
OK.

159
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:56,400
And so that means that rather than this thing here

160
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:00,680
being true monopoly profits, that actually we

161
00:07:00,680 --> 00:07:03,040
can go and think about this as yet another kind

162
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:05,960
of deadweight loss.

163
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:08,840
That whole thing can become deadweight loss.

164
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:10,600
Right?

165
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:14,320
So if there's a million dollars worth of benefit

166
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:17,120
from getting the monopoly grant, then in equilibrium

167
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:20,080
it seems like the total amount that people spend on lobbying

168
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:22,320
will be a million dollars.

169
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:23,920
Now, of course, the firm that wins

170
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:25,680
is going to do way better than that.

171
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:28,160
But on the other hand, the firms that lose

172
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:29,680
are going to be losing money.

173
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:32,560
I mean, it's just like, say, a patent race, where

174
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:35,080
whoever gets there first and wins,

175
00:07:35,080 --> 00:07:37,440
they're going to be making good profits.

176
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:39,320
But when you think about the social cost,

177
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:41,600
you also need to subtract out all of the research

178
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:44,960
efforts of all the losers who get no gross profits

179
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:47,520
and still had all the research costs.

180
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:48,000
Right?

181
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,200
And in equilibrium, we need to have price equals average cost.

182
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:53,920
So the total amount of resources that people

183
00:07:53,920 --> 00:07:56,400
spend trying to get the monopoly profit

184
00:07:56,400 --> 00:08:00,320
will actually equal the monopoly profits.

185
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:02,480
So anyway, this is a lot like mixed value reasoning.

186
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:04,640
So in equilibrium, the payoffs of production and redistribution

187
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:05,640
are going to be equal.

188
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:07,120
And you keep entering this arms race

189
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:08,880
until the net profits of the privilege

190
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:12,640
are zero, which happens when the total cost of lobbying

191
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:15,400
equals the total value of the monopoly privilege.

192
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:17,200
And this has a special name.

193
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:19,720
So again, if this doesn't need to happen,

194
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:23,320
so if that does happen, then we call it full rent dissipation.

195
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:31,080
And this here has a special name.

196
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:32,240
It's not just a rectangle.

197
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:36,880
It's the telec rectangle.

198
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:38,280
Geometrically, the telec rectangle

199
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:40,760
is exactly the same as the monopoly profit rectangle,

200
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:45,440
only we now can reinterpret it as being deadweight loss

201
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:48,640
rather than transfer.

202
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:49,120
Right?

203
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:51,120
And now, look at how gloomy this market is.

204
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:53,840
We started off with a market where the total social surplus

205
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:56,080
was this giant triangle.

206
00:08:56,080 --> 00:08:58,640
And we end up with this little stub.

207
00:08:58,640 --> 00:09:03,080
We end up with that little stub of surplus at the end.

208
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:07,160
So this is what's left over after an incompetent firm

209
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:09,320
jacks up the price after fighting

210
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:10,320
for the right to do so.

211
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:14,640
And then, yeah, there's still something.

212
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:16,520
So there's still better to have a product that

213
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:17,760
exists than not exist.

214
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:22,160
But the social gain that we just realized

215
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:26,160
when we compare it to the potential is very small.

216
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:31,040
So there, I guess it looks like 1 9th, something like that.

217
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:32,640
As drawn, these are similar triangles.

218
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:34,600
You can go and do a little bit like that.

219
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:39,440
All right.

220
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:40,720
Questions?

221
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:41,220
Yes?

222
00:09:41,220 --> 00:09:45,120
So that whole bottom trapezoid, that's all productive?

223
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:47,160
Yes, that's all productive inefficiency, exactly.

224
00:09:47,160 --> 00:09:52,040
So is this more of a competitive market?

225
00:09:52,040 --> 00:09:54,320
Yes, so the idea is that in a competitive market,

226
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:57,200
you could have this thing could have happened.

227
00:09:57,200 --> 00:09:57,760
Yes.

228
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:00,680
So again, I suppose if there were two firms that

229
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:03,920
both had that, or if one firm had these costs,

230
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:06,320
but there was a potential one that was just as efficient,

231
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:08,560
then you actually could very well be right there.

232
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:15,160
Now, when people think about this situation,

233
00:10:15,160 --> 00:10:17,240
common reaction is, well, we wouldn't

234
00:10:17,240 --> 00:10:19,720
have to go and deal with this problem.

235
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:22,560
It wouldn't be a deadweight loss of the tele-rectangle

236
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:26,800
if instead of having lobbying, we just did competitive bidding.

237
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:32,080
So suppose that instead of having a giant argument or fight

238
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:34,920
about who gets the monopoly profits,

239
00:10:34,920 --> 00:10:38,080
instead we just had the government auction it.

240
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:40,120
Then the government would raise this revenue,

241
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:42,040
and that would be a transfer to the government

242
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:45,720
rather than a deadweight loss to society.

243
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:48,360
OK, now, what Tulloch pointed out,

244
00:10:48,360 --> 00:10:53,240
though, is that there really is a law of conservation

245
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:56,760
of rent-seeking, because if the government is able to go and get

246
00:10:56,760 --> 00:10:58,880
all these monopoly profits, this is just

247
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:00,640
going to increase competition to become

248
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:02,320
the people in charge of the government who

249
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:04,360
are able to get the money.

250
00:11:04,360 --> 00:11:06,520
So you can very easily see this in things

251
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:11,760
like medieval monarchies, where on the one hand,

252
00:11:11,760 --> 00:11:14,280
if you are the king, you get all this great stuff.

253
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:17,840
But that means that other people look at you with envy

254
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:20,720
and say, if only I could become the king.

255
00:11:20,720 --> 00:11:22,320
I could be the king.

256
00:11:22,320 --> 00:11:23,600
I should be the king.

257
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:25,840
If I would just raise an army and kill

258
00:11:25,840 --> 00:11:27,800
the people in charge, that would be me.

259
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:29,880
Of course, you're not the only one thinking that.

260
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:31,680
There's a lot of people thinking that.

261
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:35,000
So if you know the show or books Game of Thrones,

262
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,720
there's enormous surplus.

263
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:38,320
But basically, it looks like it's

264
00:11:38,320 --> 00:11:40,200
all going to be dissipated.

265
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:42,480
There's going to be the whole area will be totally laid

266
00:11:42,480 --> 00:11:43,600
waste.

